# Bavarian (Boarisch) content catalog: the prose the core computes into the
# document. Selected by `documentLocale` — the language the activity was
# written in.
#
# UNREVIEWED SEED. Machine-generated, pending review by a speaker (#1521).
# Correct anything here freely; nothing in it was written by a translator.
#
# **Script and orthography.** Latin script, German-based spelling, following
# the practice of the **Bavarian Wikipedia (bar.wikipedia)** in its **Central
# Bavarian / Austro-Bavarian** register. **Bavarian has no codified standard**
# — no orthography board, no official dictionary, no agreed norm — so this is
# one written variety among several rather than *the* spelling. Unlike
# `locales/gsw`, **ß is used**: it belongs to the German-based spelling both
# Bavaria and Austria write. See `chrome.ftl` for the full note.
#
# **Do not edit this file toward Standard German**: «ned», «is», «san», «hod»,
# «ko», «koa», «nix», «oda», «Kroas» for «Kreis», «Zoi» for «Zahl» are the
# catalog, not typos.
#
# **What is borrowed.** The colour words, the connectives and the everyday
# nouns are Bavarian. The geometry and chemistry vocabulary is Standard
# German, lightly respelled — that is the register a Bavarian speaker is
# schooled in, and there is no Bavarian mathematical tradition to draw on.
#
# **Counts.** CLDR has **no plural data for `bar`**, so no plural category can
# be selected: there is **no** `[zero]`, `[one]`, `[two]`, `[few]` or `[many]`
# branch anywhere in this file. Nothing here counts, so nothing is lost.
#
# **Digits.** Every number renders in Latin digits.
#
# **Adjective order and agreement.** Bavarian inflects the way German does:
# the attributive adjective goes **before** the noun and takes an ending that
# depends on the noun's gender and on the case its position governs. So the
# adjectives here are really agreed, not invariant. Every one selects on
# `$role` first and then, where it matters, on `$gender`:
#
#   standalone          no article, so the strong endings: `-a` m, `-e` f,
#                       `-s` n («a dicka Strich», «a dicke Linie»,
#                       «a dicks Quadrat»)
#   border-clause       after «mit am» / «und am», which govern the dative:
#                       `-n`, the same for every gender
#   background-clause   after «auf am», dative again: `-n`
#   text-clause         predicative, where nothing is inflected at all: the
#                       bare stem, «rod auf am gäibn Hintagrund»
#
# As in `locales/gsw`, the strong dative `-em` that Standard German writes
# («mit dickem Rand») is not available: the article is used instead, «mit am
# dickn Rand», so the dative ending is a flat `-n` and the article is written
# into the composition message.
#
# **The periodic table is left to fall back to English.** `element-name` and
# `element-anion-name` are deliberately absent. There is no published Bavarian
# list of the 118 elements, and there will not be one: chemistry in Bavaria
# and Austria is taught in Standard German out of Standard German textbooks,
# so the table a pupil actually meets is `locales/de`'s. A reviewer who wants
# these filled in should copy `locales/de` deliberately rather than have this
# seed invent dialect element names nobody writes.


